"""Design direction for genuinely model-authored short projects, not supplied UI code."""

ENTERPRISE_DESIGN = """Create Aster Travel: an exceptionally polished enterprise travel workspace, like a finished boutique design-studio product, using the exact existing hotel/booking API. The CODE and original SVG illustrations must be authored during this run; the explicitly supplied hero image is an optional asset. Functionality takes priority over decoration.

ART DIRECTION: warm off-white #f5f3ed canvas, paper-white cards, deep forest #173c34 ink and primary buttons, muted dark sage #52665d, fine #dce2d8 rules, restrained clay #b56e4e accents. Use Georgia only for large editorial headings; system sans-serif for controls/data. Distinctive typography, careful alignment, generous but efficient whitespace, 14px minimum readable body text, elegant 14px corners. No neon, purple SaaS gradients, emoji icons, fake avatars, invented ratings, fake trust logos, decorative charts or huge empty headers. The parent owns HP branding: do not redraw HP/NVIDIA marks.

COMPOSITION: a slim branded top navigation with working Overview / Collection / Reservations links to real static section IDs, a tiny fictional-inventory label and an actual reservation count. The initial laptop screen should show an intentional two-column composition: an inviting compact editorial introduction and destination collection on the left (about 68%), a premium white booking panel on the right (about 32%). Use a 40–52px editorial heading such as 'Good work. Better places.' and one short useful sentence. Keep hero height around 150–210px, not a giant landing-page billboard. The booking form must be visible without hunting through a dialog. At widths below 800px stack panels, with no horizontal page overflow.

VISUAL CRAFT: property cards need rich large landscape imagery using the supplied --fury-artwork-hero CSS background variable with varied background-position crops, not blank rectangles or simplistic geometric houses. Small original inline SVG architectural accents are optional; supplied photography-style concept artwork is the main visual. All images must stay local. Cards show actual city/name, numeric nightly price, and an obvious 'Choose stay' action that selects the real hotel's ID in the booking form. Art is illustrative and is never a photograph or proof of actual amenities. Design the booking panel with a step number, clear labeled hotel/guest/date fields, full-width 'Confirm demo booking' action and a persistent status region. Reservations below the collection use clean rows or cards with actual hotel, guest and date, useful empty/loading/error states and visible conflict feedback. Restrained 120–180ms hover/focus transitions only; respect prefers-reduced-motion.

SUPPLIED LOCAL ARTWORK: the coordinator provides window.furyArtwork.hero, a pinned data:image/webp;base64 URI of a beautiful fictional Mediterranean retreat. This is a public supplied image, NOT live image generation or a real hotel photo. Use it to make the compact hero visually striking: HTML includes img#hero-art, CSS uses object-fit:cover with readable shaded text or a split image/text layout, and app.js sets document.getElementById('hero-art').src=window.furyArtwork.hero before loading API data. Cards may use the same supplied concept image with varied object-position crops alongside original SVG details. Do not embed base64 in source, use fetch, or add URLs. Keep a CSS/inline-SVG fallback if the artwork variable is absent. Clearly label 'Live-generated application · Supplied illustrative artwork'. Imagery must not push actual inventory, filters or the booking form out of a useful laptop layout. Inputs need accessible high-contrast labels; muted text must still meet 4.5:1 on its actual background.

FUNCTIONAL CONTRACT: GET /hotels and GET /bookings through window.furyApi ONLY, real dynamic counts, city filtering from actual inventory, numeric price sorting and a working accessible booking form. Both 200/201 are success. Display and preserve real 409 conflict feedback; never let an old success timer erase a newer error. Fetch inventory before rendering reservations or rerender once both settle; never show Unknown Hotel from a race. Match integer/string IDs consistently. Escape all untrusted text and convert numeric values safely. Preserve all established IDs, classes, native form semantics, keyboard focus and API contract during every refinement. Sidebar/top-nav active states must track real navigation. No extra routes, auth, payment, availability, spend, integrations or statistics the backend cannot supply. Fictional inventory / architectural concept illustrations / no real booking must be clearly stated.

OUTPUT DISCIPLINE: the complete HTML/CSS/JS bundle must stay within 18000 UTF-8 bytes so later complete-file repairs fit the existing context/output limits. Favor excellent reusable CSS and compact original SVG over repetition. HTML links styles.css and app.js; keep each language in its owned file. Every later task preserves this visual system and all working interactions. A beautiful static mockup cannot pass acceptance.
"""

